---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import HeroSection from "../components/Sections/HeroSection.astro";
import EventList from "../components/Sections/EventList.astro";
import Button from "../components/UI/Button.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Ariane Mader | ADHD & Procrastination Coaching"
  seoTitle="ADHD & Procrastination Coach | Amsterdam, Almere & Online | Ariane Mader"
  description="ADHD and procrastination coaching in English and German. Practical, individual support online and in Amsterdam or Almere. No diagnosis required."
  canonicalUrl="https://www.arianemader.com/"
  lang="en"
  xDefault="https://www.arianemader.com/"
>
<HeroSection
  title="ADHD & Procrastination Coaching"
  mobileTitleLine1="ADHD & Procrastination"
  mobileTitleLine2="Coaching"
  subtitle="Amsterdam • Almere • Online  |  English • German"
  backgroundImage="/uploads/home-header.png"
/>


<section id="intro-message" class="py-8 bg-white">
  <div class="w-full">
    <div class="px-6 md:px-20 lg:px-40">
      <div class="mb-3">
        <h2 class="blueFont font-[Cambria] text-2xl font-bold">
          You know exactly what to do. But you just can’t get yourself to do it.
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none [&>p]:my-2">
        <p>
          You start important things at the last minute — even when you genuinely care about them. 
          Routines don’t stick, everything feels urgent, and “trying harder” only works for a few days. 
          You’re stressed, overwhelmed, and frustrated that you’re not living up to your potential.
        </p>
        <p>
          <span class="greenDarkFont">You’re not undisciplined or lazy. You just haven’t yet found ways of working that truly fit your brain.</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          I'm an ADHD & Procrastination Coach offering online and in-person sessions in Amsterdam and Almere. 
          Together, we figure out what keeps getting in your way and experiment with practical methods to help you <b>follow through on what matters</b> without stress, guilt, or last-minute panic. 
          Whether you are neurodivergent (e.g. ADHD and autism) or simply find that mainstream productivity advice has never worked for you, we’ll figure out what does (no diagnosis needed).
        </p>
        <p>
          <a
            href="/contact"
            class="font-medium underline transition-colors text-[#193472] hover:!text-[#30B28E]"
          >Book</a>
          a free intro call.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- General -->
<section id="problem" class="py-8 bg-gray-50">
  <div class="w-full px-6 md:px-20 lg:px-40">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-[2fr_1fr] gap-12 items-center">
      <!-- Text -->
        <div class="max-w-3xl prose prose-lg">
          <h2 class="blueFont font-[Cambria] text-2xl font-bold !mt-0 !mb-3">Does this sound familiar?</h2>
            
          <p>
            You might recognize situations like these:
          </p>
          <ul class="green-bullets space-y-1 [&>li]:my-0">
            <li>You open your laptop to start... and a few hours later you are still sitting there, waiting to build up enough pressure to finally get going.</li>
            <li>You make a plan for the week, but by the afternoon it’s already falling apart.</li>
            <li>You avoid small admin tasks until they become mini-crises.</li>
            <li>You start new routines with enthusiasm, but they disappear the moment life gets busy.</li>
            <li>You keep buying new apps and planners in an attempt to regain control.</li>
            <li>You feel guilty for not doing “simple” things like laundry, taxes, or replying to someone.</li>
            <!--<li>You feel capable of more, but cannot seem to access that potential.</li>-->
            <!--•	#You spend more time thinking about the task than actually doing it.-->
          </ul>

          <p>
            Instead of listening to the resistance, most people try to push through it — because the world keeps telling us it <i>should</i> be possible that way.
          </p>
        </div>

      <!-- Image -->
      <div class="flex items-center justify-center">
        <img
          src="/uploads/home-image.jpg"
          alt="Skeleton overwhelmed by tasks covered under books at a desk"
          class="w-full h-auto object-contain rounded-xl"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>



<section id="solution" class="py-8 bg-white">
  <div class="w-full">
    <div class="px-6 md:px-20 lg:px-40">
      <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row gap-12 items-center">

        <!-- Text -->
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none [&>p]:my-2">
          <h2 class="blueFont font-[Cambria] text-2xl font-bold !mt-0 !mb-3">
            Why "just try harder" doesn't work
          </h2>
            <p>
              Most productivity advice assumes that once you know what to do, you should be able to make yourself do it.
            </p>
            <p>
              But people differ in how easily they can get started, prioritize, switch between tasks, stay focused, organize themselves and follow through — skills often grouped under the term <i>executive functioning</i>.
              So if you’ve tried harder, made better plans, downloaded more apps, and still ended up stuck, adding even more pressure probably isn’t the answer.
            </p>
            <p>
              Instead of trying harder, we find ways of working that make it easier for you to follow through without relying on pressure.
            </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

